{
  "content": "- **Reconstruct the task spec only from user messages, and treat assistant wording as provisional.** Build the running spec from explicit user text alone; do not let inferred intent, \u201chelpful\u201d reframing, or assistant assumptions become binding unless later user turns confirm them.\n\n- **Update the spec incrementally as user shards arrive, then re-check prior assistant actions against the cumulative spec available at that point.** The key question is \u201cwhat changed?\u201d: later user disclosures can validate, invalidate, or sharpen earlier interpretations, so evaluate each assistant step against the latest consolidated user-derived requirements, not in isolation.\n\n- **Track each user constraint separately and verify coverage across all of them.** A response can be partly aligned while still failing a critical filter, field, ordering, or condition; don\u2019t collapse partial correctness into full success just because the topic is roughly right.\n\n- **Treat omissions as \u201cnot yet specified,\u201d not errors, until the user actually states the constraint.** In sharded conversations, missing details are ordinary progression, not a reason to fault the assistant for not guessing or for not asking questions.\n\n- **Use contradiction detection as a primary signal.** Flag divergence when the assistant adds unsupported constraints, changes the output shape, narrows or widens the task without authorization, or otherwise introduces a meaning the user did not state.\n\n- **Watch for overcommitment and anchoring early.** If the assistant commits to a concrete interpretation, answer, or method before all user requirements are known, mark that as a risk and re-test it against later user shards; stale early framing is a common source of downstream divergence.\n\n- **Separate alignment from completeness.** Preserve the parts of the assistant response that genuinely match user-stated requirements, but isolate missing or conflicting pieces explicitly rather than treating the whole response as wrong.\n\n- **Treat answer-shape constraints as first-class requirements, not cosmetic details.** Output form, required fields, ordering, grouping, identifiers, or selection criteria can be as important as topical correctness; a response on the right subject can still diverge badly if the shape is wrong.\n\n- **Check semantic conditions, not surface wording.** Compare the assistant\u2019s approach to the user\u2019s actual logical constraints, including filters, ranges, conjunctions/disjunctions, and selection criteria, rather than to similar-sounding phrasing.\n\n- **Re-evaluate earlier \u201cgood enough\u201d work after later turns reveal more constraints.** A step that was acceptable under an earlier partial spec may become incorrect once the user adds new requirements; the analyzer should judge the trajectory by the latest complete user-visible spec.\n\n- **Do not treat clarification-seeking as the default fix for missing details.** In these trajectories, the analyzer should assume the assistant can proceed with available shards and should judge whether it did so productively, rather than treating unanswered gaps as failure by themselves.\n\n- **When the assistant drifts, prefer compacting away unsupported reasoning and retaining only verified progress.** S2-style context edits should preserve confirmed user constraints and genuinely aligned results, while discarding speculative rationale that could bias the next step.\n\n- **Separate \u201cwhat changed\u201d into concrete divergence categories.** Good issue statements identify whether the problem is a missing constraint, extra assumption, premature commitment, wrong interpretation, or stale reasoning after a new user shard; vague quality critiques are less useful than user-facing mismatch descriptions.\n\n- **Preserve transferable structure in the analysis.** When recording a failure or success, capture the general pattern behind it\u2014anchoring, omission, contradiction, spec drift, or incomplete coverage\u2014so the lesson applies across domains instead of only to the current task.\n\n- **In successful partial outcomes, identify the stable anchors that kept the assistant aligned.** Positive signals are often the assistant\u2019s continued use of user-stated constraints despite new shards; these anchors are worth preserving because they explain why the trajectory stayed on track.\n\n- **Treat the conversation state as editable when an early assumption is clearly wrong.** The analyzer should be willing to discard mistaken context and rebuild from the verified task spec plus any confirmed aligned progress, rather than carrying forward a flawed interpretation.\n\n- **Favor conservative alignment judgments over helpful-sounding leniency.** Extra goals, optimization criteria, alternative framings, or adjacent tasks still count as drift if the user did not ask for them, even when they seem reasonable or useful.\n\n- **When the user reveals information incrementally, assess whether the assistant integrated the new facts into the next step.** Failure to absorb fresh user details is often the key divergence signal, especially in short or sharded conversations where each turn materially changes the spec.\n\n- **Keep the analyzer focused on deviation detection, not general quality review.** Prioritize whether the assistant answered the same task the user asked, satisfied all explicit constraints, and avoided unsupported additions; do not over-penalize stylistic differences that do not change the requirement match.",
  "version": 2,
  "history": [
    "",
    "<updated_cheatsheet>\n- **Start by reconstructing the task spec only from user messages.** Treat assistant wording, inferred intent, and \u201chelpful\u201d reframing as non-authoritative unless later user turns explicitly confirm them. The analyzer\u2019s first job is to separate user-stated constraints from everything else.\n\n- **Treat user disclosure as incremental and time-sensitive.** After each new user shard, update the running spec by adding newly stated constraints while preserving earlier ones unless the user changes them. Re-check prior assistant work against the cumulative user-visible spec at that point in the trajectory, not against later revelations.\n\n- **Distinguish omission from contradiction.** If a constraint has not yet been stated, its absence should be treated as \u201cnot yet specified,\u201d not as an error. Flag divergence when the assistant ignores, violates, or invents beyond a constraint that is already in the user-derived spec.\n\n- **Separate \u201caligned so far\u201d from \u201cstill incomplete.\u201d** A response can be partially correct even if it does not cover the full revealed task. Preserve the useful aligned fragments in the analysis, and isolate only the missing or conflicting parts instead of collapsing the whole response into failure.\n\n- **Look for overcommitment to an early interpretation.** If the assistant locks onto one reading of the task before all user details arrive, treat that as anchoring risk and re-test whether later user turns invalidate the earlier framing. This is especially important in sharded-disclosure conversations.\n\n- **Check for specification drift introduced by the assistant.** Adding extra goals, subgoals, scope, formatting, optimization criteria, or a different problem framing is a meaningful divergence even when it sounds helpful. Prefer conservative alignment checks: helpful-sounding additions still count as drift if the user did not ask for them.\n\n- **Compare against the latest cumulative constraints, not just the latest turn.** The correct benchmark is the full user-derived spec available at that point in the conversation. A move that looked fine earlier may become misaligned once later user shards add new constraints.\n\n- **Treat the conversation state as editable when the assistant has clearly diverged.** If prior reasoning is built on a wrong assumption, prefer compacting away the mistaken context and retaining only the verified task spec plus already-aligned progress. This reduces anchoring on bad premises and lets the next turn restart cleanly.\n\n- **Use action-oriented issue statements.** Each flagged issue should clearly indicate what changed: whether the analysis should append newly disclosed constraints, discard an unsupported assumption, or replace the current state with a cleaned-up spec. Avoid vague critiques that do not tell downstream analysis what needs to be corrected.\n\n- **Prioritize contradictions and unsupported additions over stylistic differences.** The analyzer\u2019s purpose is deviation detection, not general quality review. Focus on whether the assistant answered a different task, violated an explicit requirement, or prematurely solved a subset while missing later constraints.\n\n- **Do not treat clarification-seeking as a substitute for progress in sharded settings.** Evaluate whether the assistant could continue productively with the disclosed facts instead of assuming missing details block the task. The user is a source of facts, not necessarily a conversation partner who will fill every gap.\n\n- **When judging a trajectory, preserve transferable structure in the analysis.** Record the general pattern that caused divergence\u2014constraint loss, anchoring, omission, contradiction, or spec drift\u2014so the takeaway applies across domains rather than only to the current task.\n\n- **In successful partial outcomes, identify what kept the assistant anchored to user constraints.** These stable anchors are often the most reusable positive signals: they show how the assistant stayed aligned as new user shards arrived and what should be preserved in future analyses.\n\n- **Keep the analysis compact and preserve only verified progress when editing context.** If a turn is partly correct and partly wrong, retain the correct fragments and discard the unsupported reasoning, so the next assistant step is not biased by earlier mistakes while still benefiting from genuine progress."
  ],
